  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe 09Z/3 am surface maps shows a 1035-1037 mb Canadian high \npressure over central and southern MN and ridging into Iowa and\nsw IL. Veil of high/cirrus clouds were spreading eastward into\nsw and west central IL from IA/MO early this morning, while\nnearly clear skies still over central and ne CWA. Cold temps in\nthe mid to upper 20s over central IL and lower 30s in southeast\nIL from I-70 south, with north to NW winds under 10 mph. \n\nDry and Seasonably Cold Weather into Early Wed Morning...\nCanadian high pressure to sprawl eastward across the Great \nLakes region today, and into New England overnight into Wed \nwhile still ridging back into the Great Lakes. High pressure \nshould keep central/se IL dry through at least early Wed morning\nwith north winds turning NE later this morning and prevailing \nthrough Wed at 5-15 mph. High clouds to continue to increase \nfrom the west during today as skies become mostly cloudy. Highs \ntoday mostly in the lower 40s over central IL and mid 40s \nsoutheast IL. Galesburg may struggle to reach 40F. Lows tonight \nin the mid to upper 20s. Highs Wed and Thu in the lower 40s \nacross the CWA, with brisk north to nw winds making it feel much\ncolder on Thanksgiving Day with the precipitation.\n\nWintry Mix of Rain and Snow Wednesday Afternoon into Thursday \nNight... A pair of strong shortwaves are progged to move across\nthe Central US and lower MS river valley midweek. The northern \nstream wave will dig down over western MO by early Wednesday \nevening while strong upper level trof/low will be near the LA \ndelta/gulf coast. An associated inverted surface trof to develop\nnorthward from the central Gulf Coast into TN/KY tonight and \nWed morning and pivot nw into east central/ne IL during Wed \nnight along with a SE low level jet and frontogenetic \nforcing/lift into eastern and ne CWA. Models showing rain \nspreading northward into southeast IL during late Wed \nmorning/early Wed afternoon and into central and east central IL\nduring mid/late Wed afternoon. This system will have plenty of \ndeeper gulf and even Atlantic moisture to work with and models \nhave continued to trend higher with QPF over central and se IL, \nwith NBM having 1-1.6 inches of qpf over east central and \nsoutheast IL from Wed afternoon thru Thu night (with quarter to \nhalf inch qpf west of the IL river). Models continue to offer \ndiffering solutions even from their previous runs regarding \nprecipitation type and possible snow accumulations (how much \nsnow). Case in point, the latest 06z HRRR model has shifted the \nheavier snow band further west over western half of CWA for Wed \nevening while previous runs were further east between the IL \nriver and I-57. In addition to how far trof gets into IL Wed \nnight (milder east of trof), borderline temperature thresholds \nand how much dynamic cooling we get along with relatively milder\nsoil temperatures to make this a very difficult snowfall \nforecast. Consensus has best chances of mixed pcpn and snow \naccumulations west of I-57 Wed night into early Thu morning. NBM\nand WPC have 1-3 inches of snow from I-57 west with 25% chance \nof seeing 3-5 inch snow bands west of I-57 to along I-55 Wed \nnight and early Thu morning. \n\nModerating Temperatures (This Weekend through Early Next Week)...\nThe two strong cutoff 500 mb lows to merge into one large \nsystem as it ejects into New England Thu night and Friday, with \nrain/mixed pcpn diminishing from the west by overnight Thu night\ninto Fri morning and low clouds decreasing from the west on \nFriday. Highs Fri in the low to mid 40s. Semi zonal upper level \nflow develops over the country this weekend, allowing \ntemperatures to moderate back to near or above seasonal levels \nwith highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s Saturday thru Tuesday. \nThere is a upper level trof passing through IL around Sunday \nwhich could bring chance of rain showers late Sat night and \nSunday. GFS model is dry over IL then but ECMWF and GEM models \nare wet on Sunday over CWA.\n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Dec\n1-7th has 70-80% chance of above normal temperatures over IL \nand 40-50% chance of above normal precipitation.",
